#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ccfleet -- run headless Claude Code sessions across a fleet of repos, each
one billed to its own repo, started only while the machine has headroom.

Why it exists: per-repo cost tools (`ccrepo`) label usage by the session's
working-directory basename. A subagent started with the Agent tool runs in its
parent's directory, so work done "for" another repo by an Agent subagent bills
the orchestrator's repo, not the repo it was for. The fix is structural: each
repo's work runs as its own `claude -p` session started INSIDE a worktree of
that repo whose basename is the repo name. Doing that by hand for a dozen repos,
without crashing the machine, is what this instrument does.

THE LESSONS IT CARRIES (each one paid for in a real run):
  * `claude -p` is always started with stdin from /dev/null. Without it a
    "no stdin" warning lands in the JSON log and the result cannot be parsed.
  * Every launch names its model (`--model`). A launch without one silently
    inherits the default tier; tools/modelguard.py blocks such launches.
  * The worktree's basename is the repo name, under a per-job parent directory
    (`<worktrees_root>/<label>-<HHMM>/<repo>`). That basename is the billing key.
  * Never repo work as an Agent subagent of the orchestrator (see above).
  * A public repo's prompt never names a private repo. Rendering refuses to
    launch a prompt for a public (or unknown-visibility) repo that contains any
    configured private name wherever no letter or digit touches it, so a
    <label>-<HHMM> folder, a name-kit folder, name_notes and atelier--name are
    caught. The guard is a literal match, so it catches the names you list and
    nothing you forgot to list.
  * This is Python, not zsh, on purpose: zsh reads a script as it runs, so
    editing a driver script mid-run broke every running job's tail. Python
    compiles the whole file at start, so a running driver is immune; a job
    started after an edit runs the edited copy.
  * Start one job at a time, staggered, and only while load, free memory and
    free disk all have headroom: parallel test runs inside headless sessions
    have taken a 16 GB laptop past load 100. Workers run their own tests
    niced and single-process; say so in the prompt.
  * An end is clean only when the session exited 0 and its JSON flags no
    error; anything else reads as ERROR (TIMEOUT at the time limit) whatever
    its text says, so a cut-off pass is never read as DONE. A worktree is
    removed only after a clean RESULT, never by force, and never while it
    holds uncommitted files or commits on no remote branch. The drain stops
    after three unclean ends in a row, which is how a spent budget shows up
    from outside.
  * A new session is given its id up front (--session-id), so a session
    killed before it prints still has one on its START line and its claim.
  * A session still running at session_timeout_minutes (default 480) is
    ended; a stopping driver reports a hung child instead of waiting forever.
  * Every session runs with CLAUDE_CODE_PRINT_BG_WAIT_CEILING_MS=0. Without
    it `claude -p` kills a background subagent about ten minutes after the
    orchestrator's last turn, so a reviewer working on act 2 died mid-act.
  * `claude -p` prints only the last turn. A session whose last turn followed
    a background notification printed no RESULT line though its report was
    written; the harvest then reads the session's own transcript, on a clean
    end only, taking the newest text that has a RESULT line and an ITEM: or
    REPO: line (the final-output shape), never a line of narration.
  * A `touch <kit_dir>/stop` ends launching cleanly; running jobs finish. A
    stop file older than the driver is ignored (and logged): last week's file
    once ended a new run before it started anything.
  * The ceiling is live: <kit_dir>/max holds it while a driver runs; edit the
    number to pace a run without stopping it.
  * One driver (drive or drain) per kit_dir: a second is refused while the
    first lives (flock), and children a dead driver started still hold slots.
  * A dry drain writes its own launched list and calls no queue.ranked, so a
    rehearsal never makes the real drain read a full queue as empty. A pick
    that ends before its session starts is taken back off the list.
  * Children start in their own session, so closing the driver's terminal
    does not end the sessions it started.
  * The config is the whole grant. A headless session would load the
    operator's user-level settings, whose allow rules widened a config that
    narrowed the tool list (a config without python3 ran python3). Every
    launch passes --setting-sources project,local and a --settings file
    ccfleet writes: the user-level settings less their allow rules, default
    mode and extra directories, and less any hook that prints an allow
    decision; their deny and ask rules, guard hooks, plugins and env are
    kept. Leaving the user source out also leaves out the user-level
    CLAUDE.md, so ccfleet appends it to the system prompt. A repo's own
    settings files still load; a launch prints a WIDENED line for each grant.
  * A clean RESULT that leaves unlanded work is not clean: it reads as
    UNLANDED, the claim stays and the worktree is kept for a resume. A DONE
    that left uncommitted work once released its claim, tying the work to no
    queue item.

Usage:
  ccfleet check [--prompt FILE ...] [--set K=V ...]
                                      validate the config; name placeholders nothing fills
  ccfleet gate                        print the headroom reading; exit 0 if a start may go now
  ccfleet run REPO --prompt FILE [--label L] [--model M] [--set K=V ...] [--keep]
                                      [--in WT [--item ITEM]] [--dry-run]
  ccfleet drive JOBS [--max N] [--serial-per-repo] [--day YYYY-MM-DD] [--dry-run]
  ccfleet drain [--max N] [--dry-run]
  ccfleet pass REPO ITEM BRIEF [--dry-run]      one drain pass (drain starts these)
  ccfleet resume REPO WORKTREE SESSION --prompt FILE [--item ITEM] [--set K=V ...]
  common: --config PATH (else $CCFLEET_CONFIG)  --by ID (else $CCFLEET_BY): the
  orchestrating session's id, filled into prompts as {BY}.

JOBS is a TSV file, one job per line: repo, label, prompt template, then any
number of KEY=VALUE fields that fill {KEY} in that template (MODEL=<id> sets the
model; a value written @file:PATH takes that file's text, for a multi-line
task). '#' lines and blank lines are skipped. Every label must be its own: a
file whose labels repeat is refused before anything starts, since the label
keys the result file. A job whose result file for the drive's day records a
clean RESULT is skipped, so re-running a drive resumes it (--day names the
day of a drive begun before midnight).

CONFIG is JSON (see instruments/ccfleet.example.json, every value fictional):
  kit_dir         logs/, results/<date>/, scratch/<repo>/, the stop file   (required)
  repos_root      primary checkouts live at <repos_root>/<repo>          (required)
  worktrees_root  job worktrees go under here                            (required)
  default_model   the model a job runs on when neither job nor repo names one (required)
  claude_bin      default "claude";   claude_args  extra args, e.g. a permission mode
                  (the narrowest that does the work: `check` names any set; never
                  --setting-sources, --settings or --append-system-prompt[-file])
  settings        Claude Code settings every session gets on top of the user-level
                  ones less their grants: with claude_args, the whole grant
  drop_hooks      texts: a user-level hook whose command holds one is left out
  session_timeout_minutes  default 480; 0 for no limit
  nice            default 5;  max_concurrent  default 4;  stagger_seconds  default 90;  poll_seconds 30
  gate            {max_load (default 2 x cores), min_mem_pct 25, min_disk_gb 20}
  reset_utc       "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM": no new start at or after it (a budget reset)
  private_names   names a public repo's prompt must never contain
  vars            template variables for every job (the principal's words, paths)
  env             extra environment variables for every session (NAME: value)
  repos           {name: {model, notes, visibility, github}}  per-repo facts
  queue           drain only: {next, ranked, take, release[, ended]} argv lists, and
                  release_on / stop_on lists of RESULT words (see below)
  pass_prompt     drain only: the per-pass orchestrator prompt template
  pass_model      drain only: the orchestrator's model (the reviewer's tier is the prompt's business)

THE QUEUE INTERFACE (drain). The review queue itself is the adopter's own tool;
ccfleet only calls it. Each entry is an argv list; {REPO} {ITEM} {BRIEF} {BY}
{WT} {LOG} are filled in before the call.
  Each is given QUEUE_TIMEOUT (600) seconds; one that cannot start or overruns
  counts as a failed call.
  next     prints "REPO ITEM BRIEF_RELPATH" for the next pass to run, or nothing
           when the queue is empty; tab-separated when a field may hold a
           space. It is called with the launched list's path in {LAUNCHED}
           (one tab-separated "REPO ITEM BRIEF" per line) so it can skip them.
  ranked   exits 0 only when the whole queue, every repo's, has been ranked
           and routed for this run (the review-drain skill's first step);
           called once before the first pick, with {BY}, after the reset
           check and never by a dry drain. Any other exit starts nothing: the
           drain prints "DRAIN EXIT: not ranked" and exits 1.
  take     claims the pass, with {SESSION} (the id the session will have);
           a nonzero exit means someone else holds it (skip).
  release  frees the claim after a pass ends with a RESULT in release_on
           (default DONE, SKIPPED) and nothing unlanded (else UNLANDED: no
           release); a refusal is printed, keeps the worktree and exits 2
           (pass, resume, run --item).
  ended    optional: called after every pass, resume or run --item, any
           RESULT, with {SESSION} {RESULT} {LOG} {WT} filled (to record them).
  stop_on  RESULT words that stop the drain (default BUDGET EXHAUSTED).

Template variables always filled: {REPO} {GH} {BR} {VIS} {PIN} {WT} {PRIMARY}
{LANDING} {NOTES} {SCRATCH} {BY} {DATE} {SESSION}, plus config `vars`, plus the
job's own. `ccfleet check` names any placeholder of pass_prompt (or a --prompt)
that nothing fills.
{PIN}, when the template uses it and nothing supplies it, is the repo's atelier
pin (tools/pins.py, else the CLAUDE.md atelier@<sha>), else UNRESOLVED.
A pass adds {ITEM} {BRIEF} {REVIEW_LOG} (<kit_dir>/review-logs/<repo>-<item>-<hash>.md,
the reviewer's private log; the same path for its resume and fold; a log an
earlier pass left there is moved, dated, to review-logs/withdrawn/ first); a
resume adds {ITEM} and, with --item, {REVIEW_LOG}; run --in WT --item ITEM (a
fold) adds {ITEM} and {REVIEW_LOG} too.
A {UPPER_CASE} placeholder left unfilled is a refusal, not a silent gap.

Exit codes: 0 ran (the session's own verdict is in its END line and result
file) · 1 setup failed (or, for drain: not ranked, the queue's next failed, or
every pick ended before its session started) · 2 claim refused (another
session holds it, or another resume of that session runs) or a release refused
(pass, resume, run --item) · 3 reset reached · 4 queue empty (a drain that
started nothing) · 5 refused by the private-name guard or an unfilled
placeholder (check: a template has one) · 64 usage (incl. an unreadable or
malformed config, a repeated jobs label, --set with no '=', a repo given as a
path). See `man ccfleet`, THREATS, for what can steer an unattended session.
Runtime: Python 3.8 or later, standard library only; git on PATH; gh optional
(visibility lookup when a repo's config does not state it).
"""
import argparse
import datetime as dt
import fcntl
import glob
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import uuid

EXIT_SETUP, EXIT_CLAIM, EXIT_RESET, EXIT_EMPTY, EXIT_GUARD, EXIT_USAGE = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 64
PLACEHOLDER = re.compile(r"\{([A-Z][A-Z0-9_]*)\}")
# A RESULT line: markdown bold or backticks around the word are tolerated, and
# every capital-letter word in a row is captured; result_word() then keeps a
# known phrase (BUDGET EXHAUSTED) or the first word, so "SKIPPED NEEDS LIVE
# WRITE" reads SKIPPED and "DONE WITH FINDINGS" reads DONE.
RESULT_RX = re.compile(r"RESULT:[\s*`]*([A-Z]+(?:[ \t]+[A-Z]+)*)")
# The final-output shape's identity line: the transcript fallback takes only a
# text that carries one, so a line of planning narration is never a verdict.
SHAPE_RX = re.compile(r"^\s*\**(?:ITEM|REPO):", re.M)
PIN_RX = re.compile(r"atelier@([0-9a-f]{7,40})\b", re.I)
# Without this, `claude -p` kills a background subagent about ten minutes after
# the orchestrator's last turn: a reviewer still working on act 2 dies mid-act.
CEILING_VAR = "CLAUDE_CODE_PRINT_BG_WAIT_CEILING_MS"
RELEASE_ON = ["DONE", "SKIPPED"]
# A clean RESULT whose worktree still holds unlanded work reads as this word,
# which no release_on may hold: the claim stays and the pass can be resumed.
UNLANDED = "UNLANDED"
STOP_ON = ["BUDGET EXHAUSTED"]
TIMEOUT_RC = 124          # the exit code a session killed at its time limit is given
QUEUE_TIMEOUT = 600       # seconds a queue command may take before it counts as failed
# Values ccfleet fills in every prompt, and those only a pass or a resume adds
# (`check` uses these to name the placeholders nothing fills).
ALWAYS_FILLED = {"REPO", "GH", "BR", "VIS", "PIN", "WT", "PRIMARY", "LANDING", "NOTES", "SCRATCH",
                 "BY", "DATE", "SESSION"}
PASS_FILLED = {"ITEM", "BRIEF", "REVIEW_LOG"}
RESUME_FILLED = {"ITEM", "REVIEW_LOG"}
# claude_args that widen what an unattended session may do without asking.
PERMISSION_FLAGS = ("--dangerously-skip-permissions", "--allow-dangerously-skip-permissions",
                    "--permission-mode", "--allowedTools", "--allowed-tools", "--add-dir")


class Refusal(Exception):
    def __init__(self, code, msg):
        super().__init__(msg)
        self.code = code


def now_utc():
    return dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc)


def expand(p):
    return os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(p)) if p else p


# ---------- config ----------

def load_config(path):
    path = path or os.environ.get("CCFLEET_CONFIG")
    if not path:
        raise Refusal(EXIT_USAGE, "no config: pass --config PATH or set CCFLEET_CONFIG")
    try:
        with open(expand(path), encoding="utf-8") as f:
            cfg = json.load(f)
    except OSError as e:
        raise Refusal(EXIT_USAGE, f"config {path}: cannot read it ({e.strerror or e})")
    except ValueError as e:
        raise Refusal(EXIT_USAGE, f"config {path}: not valid JSON ({e})")
    if not isinstance(cfg, dict):
        raise Refusal(EXIT_USAGE, f"config {path}: must be a JSON object")
    missing = [k for k in ("kit_dir", "repos_root", "worktrees_root", "default_model") if not cfg.get(k)]
    if missing:
        raise Refusal(EXIT_USAGE, f"config {path}: missing {', '.join(missing)}")
    for k in ("kit_dir", "repos_root", "worktrees_root"):
        cfg[k] = expand(cfg[k])
    cfg.setdefault("claude_bin", "claude")
    cfg.setdefault("claude_args", [])
    cfg.setdefault("nice", 5)
    cfg.setdefault("max_concurrent", 4)
    cfg.setdefault("stagger_seconds", 90)
    cfg.setdefault("poll_seconds", 30)
    t = cfg.setdefault("session_timeout_minutes", 480)
    if not isinstance(t, (int, float)) or isinstance(t, bool) or t < 0:
        raise Refusal(EXIT_USAGE, f"config {path}: session_timeout_minutes must be a number, 0 for none")
    if not isinstance(cfg["claude_args"], list):
        raise Refusal(EXIT_USAGE, f"config {path}: claude_args must be a list of arguments")
    owned = sorted({str(a).split("=", 1)[0] for a in cfg["claude_args"]} & set(OWNED_FLAGS))
    if owned:
        raise Refusal(EXIT_USAGE, f"config {path}: claude_args may not set {', '.join(owned)}: ccfleet sets "
                                  f"them so the config is the whole grant; put settings in `settings`")
    if not isinstance(cfg.setdefault("settings", {}), dict):
        raise Refusal(EXIT_USAGE, f"config {path}: settings must be a JSON object (Claude Code settings)")
    dh = cfg.setdefault("drop_hooks", [])
    if not isinstance(dh, list) or not all(isinstance(x, str) and x for x in dh):
        raise Refusal(EXIT_USAGE, f"config {path}: drop_hooks must be a list of non-empty texts")
    if cfg.get("reset_utc"):
        try:
            parse_reset(cfg["reset_utc"])
        except (TypeError, ValueError):
            raise Refusal(EXIT_USAGE, f"config {path}: reset_utc must be YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM in UTC, "
                                      f"not {cfg['reset_utc']!r}")
    gate = cfg.setdefault("gate", {})
    gate.setdefault("max_load", 2 * (os.cpu_count() or 4))
    gate.setdefault("min_mem_pct", 25)
    gate.setdefault("min_disk_gb", 20)
    cfg.setdefault("private_names", [])
    cfg.setdefault("vars", {})
    cfg.setdefault("repos", {})
    if not isinstance(cfg.setdefault("env", {}), dict):
        raise Refusal(EXIT_USAGE, f"config {path}: env must be an object of NAME: value")
    q = cfg.get("queue") or {}
    for k, default in (("release_on", RELEASE_ON), ("stop_on", STOP_ON)):
        if not isinstance(q.get(k, default), list):
            raise Refusal(EXIT_USAGE, f"config {path}: queue.{k} must be a list of RESULT words")
    if UNLANDED in q.get("release_on", RELEASE_ON):
        raise Refusal(EXIT_USAGE, f"config {path}: queue.release_on may not hold {UNLANDED}: it is the word "
                                  f"for a clean end that left unlanded work, which keeps its claim")
    return cfg


def release_on(cfg):
    return list((cfg.get("queue") or {}).get("release_on", RELEASE_ON))


def stop_on(cfg):
    return list((cfg.get("queue") or {}).get("stop_on", STOP_ON))


def parse_reset(r):
    return dt.datetime.strptime(r, "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M").replace(tzinfo=dt.timezone.utc)


def reset_reached(cfg, at=None):
    r = cfg.get("reset_utc")
    if not r:
        return False
    return (at or now_utc()) >= parse_reset(r)


def check_repo_name(repo):
    """A repo is a plain name under repos_root, never a path: a path would miss
    its config entry and move the worktree out of its job folder."""
    if not repo or repo in (".", "..") or "/" in repo or "\0" in repo or repo != repo.strip():
        raise Refusal(EXIT_USAGE, f"repo must be a plain name under repos_root, not {repo!r}")
    return repo


def parse_sets(pairs):
    """--set K=V values; one with no '=' is a usage error, never a traceback."""
    out = {}
    for s in pairs:
        if "=" not in s:
            raise Refusal(EXIT_USAGE, f"--set {s!r}: expected K=V")
        k, v = s.split("=", 1)
        out[k] = v
    return out


def permission_args(cfg):
    """What the config grants an unattended session beyond asking (its
    claude_args flags and its `settings` permissions), and whether any of it is
    a bypass (nothing is asked at all). Since user-level settings are not
    loaded, this is the whole grant but for a repo's own settings files."""
    args, found, bypass = cfg.get("claude_args") or [], [], False
    for i, a in enumerate(args):
        a = str(a)
        flag = a.split("=", 1)[0]
        if flag not in PERMISSION_FLAGS:
            continue
        value = a.split("=", 1)[1] if "=" in a else (
            str(args[i + 1]) if flag not in PERMISSION_FLAGS[:2] and i + 1 < len(args) else "")
        found.append(f"{flag} {value}".strip())
        if flag == PERMISSION_FLAGS[0] or (flag == "--permission-mode" and value == "bypassPermissions"):
            bypass = True
    perms = (cfg.get("settings") or {}).get("permissions")
    for k in GRANT_KEYS if isinstance(perms, dict) else ():
        v = perms.get(k)
        if v:
            found.append(f"settings {k} {', '.join(map(str, v)) if isinstance(v, list) else v}")
            bypass = bypass or (k == "defaultMode" and v == "bypassPermissions")
    return found, bypass


# ---------- the session's settings: the config is the whole grant ----------
#
# A headless session would otherwise load the operator's user-level settings,
# whose allow rules, default mode and extra directories widen whatever the
# config narrows (found 2026-11-23: a config without python3 in its tool list
# ran python3 anyway). So every launch passes --setting-sources project,local,
# which leaves the user-level settings file out, and --settings with a file
# ccfleet writes: the user-level settings with their grants taken out (hooks,
# deny and ask rules, plugins and env kept: they restrict or equip, they do
# not grant), then the config's own `settings` on top. Leaving the user source
# out also leaves out the user-level CLAUDE.md (checked against claude 2.1.x),
# so ccfleet appends it to the system prompt (--append-system-prompt-file):
# the operator's standing instructions still bind an unattended session.
SETTING_SOURCES = "project,local"
# The permission keys that widen what a session may do: never carried over.
GRANT_KEYS = ("allow", "defaultMode", "additionalDirectories")
# Launch flags ccfleet sets itself: in claude_args they would undo the above.
OWNED_FLAGS = ("--setting-sources", "--settings", "--append-system-prompt", "--append-system-prompt-file")
# A hook command that prints an allow decision approves tool calls: a grant.
GRANTING_HOOK_RX = re.compile(r"permissionDecision\W{0,8}allow", re.I)


def read_settings(path, required=True):
    """A settings file as a dict; {} when it does not exist. One that cannot be
    read is a refusal when `required` (the user's: launching without it would
    silently drop its guard hooks and deny rules), else None."""
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            d = json.load(f)
        if not isinstance(d, dict):
            raise ValueError("not a JSON object")
        return d
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return {}
    except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
        if not required:
            return None
        raise Refusal(EXIT_SETUP, f"cannot read the user settings {path} ({e}): launching without them "
                                  f"would drop their hooks and deny rules")


def merge_settings(base, over):
    """`over` on top of `base`: objects merge key by key, lists join without
    repeats, any other value is `over`'s."""
    out = dict(base)
    for k, v in over.items():
        if isinstance(v, dict) and isinstance(out.get(k), dict):
            out[k] = merge_settings(out[k], v)
        elif isinstance(v, list) and isinstance(out.get(k), list):
            out[k] = out[k] + [x for x in v if x not in out[k]]
        else:
            out[k] = v
    return out


def user_settings_path():
    return os.path.join(config_dir(), "settings.json")


def hook_commands(group):
    return [str(h.get("command", "")) for h in group.get("hooks", []) if isinstance(h, dict)]


def session_settings(cfg):
    """(settings, notes): the user-level settings less their grants and less
    the hooks that grant or that the config drops, with the config's own
    `settings` on top; and what was left out, for `check` and the man page's
    account. A hook command is dropped when it prints an allow decision or
    holds a drop_hooks text; the other commands of its entry are kept."""
    user = read_settings(user_settings_path())
    notes = {"grants": [], "dropped_hooks": [], "kept_hooks": []}
    perms = user.get("permissions")
    if isinstance(perms, dict):
        perms = dict(perms)
        for k in GRANT_KEYS:
            if k in perms:
                v = perms.pop(k)
                if k == "allow":
                    notes["grants"].append(f"allow ({len(v) if isinstance(v, list) else v} rule(s))")
                else:
                    notes["grants"].append(f"{k} {', '.join(map(str, v)) if isinstance(v, list) else v}")
        user = {**user, "permissions": perms}
    hooks = user.get("hooks")
    if isinstance(hooks, dict):
        kept = {}
        for event, groups in hooks.items():
            for g in groups if isinstance(groups, list) else []:
                if not isinstance(g, dict):
                    continue
                where = f"{event} {g.get('matcher', '*') or '*'}"
                left = []
                for h in g.get("hooks", []) if isinstance(g.get("hooks"), list) else []:
                    c = str(h.get("command", "")) if isinstance(h, dict) else ""
                    drop = [d for d in cfg.get("drop_hooks", []) if d in c]
                    if GRANTING_HOOK_RX.search(c) or drop:
                        why = f"drop_hooks {drop[0]!r}" if drop else "approves tool calls"
                        notes["dropped_hooks"].append(f"{where} ({why})")
                        continue
                    left.append(h)
                    notes["kept_hooks"].append(f"{where}: {c}")
                if left:
                    kept.setdefault(event, []).append({**g, "hooks": left})
        user = {**user, "hooks": kept}
    return merge_settings(user, cfg.get("settings") or {}), notes


def session_files(cfg, sid, write=True):
    """Write (unless not `write`) the session's settings file, and the
    user-level CLAUDE.md as an instructions file when there is one, under
    <kit_dir>/settings/. Returns their paths (the second None when there is no
    user CLAUDE.md)."""
    d = os.path.join(cfg["kit_dir"], "settings")
    spath, ipath = os.path.join(d, f"{sid}.json"), os.path.join(d, f"{sid}-user-instructions.md")
    umd = os.path.join(config_dir(), "CLAUDE.md")
    if not os.path.isfile(umd):
        ipath = None
    if not write:
        return spath, ipath
    settings, _ = session_settings(cfg)
    os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
    fd = os.open(spath, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o600)
    with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        json.dump(settings, f, indent=1)
    if ipath:
        with open(umd, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
            text = f.read()
        fd = os.open(ipath, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o600)
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write(f"# The operator's user-level instructions ({umd})\n\n"
                    "ccfleet loads no user-level settings, so Claude Code does not load this file "
                    "itself; it is passed here unchanged and binds as it would have.\n\n" + text)
    return spath, ipath


def repo_grants(wt):
    """What a worktree's own settings files (the project and local sources,
    which still load) grant, one line each: the config is not the whole grant
    where these say something, and each launch says so."""
    out = []
    for rel in (os.path.join(".claude", "settings.json"), os.path.join(".claude", "settings.local.json")):
        d = read_settings(os.path.join(wt, rel), required=False)
        if d is None:
            out.append(f"{rel}: unreadable (Claude Code ignores an invalid settings file)")
            continue
        perms = d.get("permissions") if isinstance(d.get("permissions"), dict) else {}
        got = [f"{k} {json.dumps(perms[k])}" for k in GRANT_KEYS if perms.get(k)]
        hooks = d.get("hooks") if isinstance(d.get("hooks"), dict) else {}
        if any(GRANTING_HOOK_RX.search(c) for gs in hooks.values() if isinstance(gs, list)
               for g in gs if isinstance(g, dict) for c in hook_commands(g)):
            got.append("a hook that approves tool calls")
        if got:
            out.append(f"{rel}: {'; '.join(got)}")
    return out


# ---------- headroom gate ----------

def read_mem_pct():
    """Free memory as a percentage, or None when it cannot be read."""
    if sys.platform == "darwin":
        try:
            out = subprocess.run(["memory_pressure", "-Q"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20).stdout
            m = re.search(r"free percentage:\s*(\d+)", out)
            return int(m.group(1)) if m else None
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
            return None
    try:
        info = {}
        with open("/proc/meminfo") as f:
            for line in f:
                k, v = line.split(":", 1)
                info[k] = int(v.split()[0])
        return int(100 * info["MemAvailable"] / info["MemTotal"])
    except (OSError, KeyError, ValueError):
        return None


def read_headroom(cfg):
    l1, l5, l15 = os.getloadavg()
    probe = cfg["worktrees_root"]
    while not os.path.exists(probe):
        probe = os.path.dirname(probe)
    disk_gb = shutil.disk_usage(probe).free // 2**30
    return {"load": (l1, l5, l15), "mem_pct": read_mem_pct(), "disk_gb": disk_gb}


def gate_ok(gate, reading):
    """A start may go only when every reading has headroom. An unreadable
    memory figure holds (fail closed) unless min_mem_pct is 0."""
    l1, l5, _ = reading["load"]
    if l1 >= gate["max_load"] or l5 >= gate["max_load"]:
        return False
    if gate["min_mem_pct"] > 0 and (reading["mem_pct"] is None or reading["mem_pct"] < gate["min_mem_pct"]):
        return False
    return reading["disk_gb"] >= gate["min_disk_gb"]


def describe(reading):
    l = reading["load"]
    mem = "?" if reading["mem_pct"] is None else reading["mem_pct"]
    return f"load {l[0]:.1f}/{l[1]:.1f}/{l[2]:.1f} mem {mem}% disk {reading['disk_gb']}G"


# ---------- git facts ----------

def git(*args, cwd=None, check=True):
    r = subprocess.run(["git", *args], cwd=cwd, capture_output=True, text=True)
    if check and r.returncode != 0:
        raise Refusal(EXIT_SETUP, f"git {' '.join(args)}: {r.stderr.strip()}")
    return r.stdout.strip()


def repo_facts(cfg, repo, fetch=True):
    primary = os.path.join(cfg["repos_root"], repo)
    if not os.path.isdir(os.path.join(primary, ".git")) and not os.path.isfile(os.path.join(primary, ".git")):
        raise Refusal(EXIT_SETUP, f"not a git checkout: {primary}")
    rc = cfg["repos"].get(repo, {})
    remote = git("remote", "get-url", "origin", cwd=primary, check=False)
    facts = {"primary": primary, "remote": bool(remote)}
    if remote:
        if fetch:
            git("fetch", "-q", "origin", cwd=primary)
        # Not every repo's mainline is main.
        ref = git("symbolic-ref", "--short", "refs/remotes/origin/HEAD", cwd=primary, check=False)
        facts["defref"] = ref or "origin/main"
        facts["branch"] = facts["defref"].split("/", 1)[1]
        m = re.search(r"github\.com[:/](.+?)(?:\.git)?$", remote)
        facts["gh"] = rc.get("github") or (m.group(1) if m else remote)
    else:
        facts["defref"] = facts["branch"] = rc.get("branch", "main")
        facts["gh"] = rc.get("github", "(no remote)")
    vis = rc.get("visibility")
    if not vis and remote and shutil.which("gh") and fetch:
        r = subprocess.run(["gh", "repo", "view", facts["gh"], "--json", "visibility", "-q", ".visibility"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True)
        vis = r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else None
    facts["visibility"] = (vis or "UNKNOWN").upper()
    return facts


def landing_text(facts, wt, branch_name=None):
    if facts["remote"]:
        b = facts["branch"]
        return (f"Land each commit with `git -C '{wt}' pull --rebase origin {b}` then "
                f"`git -C '{wt}' push origin HEAD:{b}` (unless this repo's CLAUDE.md requires "
                f"a PR; then open one and say so).")
    return (f"This repo has NO remote; local {facts['branch']} is the mainline and your worktree is on "
            f"branch {branch_name}. Instead of pull/push: `git -C '{wt}' rebase {facts['branch']}`, then land "
            f"with `git -C '{facts['primary']}' merge --ff-only {branch_name}` against the primary checkout "
            f"(only while it is on {facts['branch']}; if the merge refuses over uncommitted changes there, "
            f"wait and retry, never stash or absorb them). Never `git push`.")


# ---------- prompt rendering ----------

def render(template, values):
    out = PLACEHOLDER.sub(lambda m: str(values[m.group(1)]) if m.group(1) in values else m.group(0), template)
    left = sorted(set(PLACEHOLDER.findall(out)))
    return out, left


def private_hits(cfg, text, visibility):
    """Private names in a prompt bound for a public or unknown-visibility repo."""
    if visibility == "PRIVATE":
        return []
    names = set(cfg["private_names"])
    names |= {r for r, rc in cfg["repos"].items() if str(rc.get("visibility", "")).upper() == "PRIVATE"}
    # Only letters and digits continue a word: a name joined by '-', '_', '.'
    # or '/' (a <label>-<HHMM> worktree parent, a -kit kit_dir, name_notes,
    # atelier--name) is still the name. "[^\W_]" is a letter or digit.
    return sorted(n for n in names if n and re.search(r"(?<![^\W_])" + re.escape(n) + r"(?![^\W_])", text, re.I))


def build_prompt(cfg, template_path, values, template_text=None):
    if template_text is None:
        template_text = read_template(template_path)
    text, left = render(template_text, values)
    if left:
        raise Refusal(EXIT_GUARD, f"unfilled placeholder(s) in {template_path}: {', '.join('{'+k+'}' for k in left)}")
    hits = private_hits(cfg, text, values.get("VIS", "UNKNOWN"))
    if hits:
        raise Refusal(EXIT_GUARD, f"refused: the prompt for {values.get('REPO')} ({values.get('VIS')}) names "
                                  f"private name(s) {', '.join(hits)}")
    return text


# ---------- one session ----------

def config_dir():
    """Claude Code's own directory: $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR, else ~/.claude."""
    return expand(os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR") or "~/.claude")


def message_text(msg):
    """The text blocks of a transcript message, joined."""
    if not isinstance(msg, dict):
        return ""
    c = msg.get("content")
    if isinstance(c, str):
        return c
    if isinstance(c, list):
        return "\n".join(b.get("text", "") for b in c if isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "text")
    return ""


def result_word(text, known=()):
    """The verdict of the last RESULT: line in `text`, or "" when there is none.
    A known phrase (e.g. BUDGET EXHAUSTED) is kept whole; otherwise the first
    capital-letter word is the verdict."""
    found = RESULT_RX.findall(text or "")
    if not found:
        return ""
    words = found[-1].split()
    for phrase in sorted((k.split() for k in known), key=len, reverse=True):
        if phrase and words[:len(phrase)] == phrase:
            return " ".join(phrase)
    return words[0]


def transcript_result(sid, since=None):
    """The newest assistant text in the session's own transcript
    (<config dir>/projects/*/<sid>.jsonl) that carries a RESULT: line AND an
    ITEM: or REPO: line of the final-output shape, or "". Entries stamped
    before `since` (ISO UTC) are not read, so a resumed session never takes an
    earlier run's verdict; a line of planning narration that mentions a
    RESULT is not a report, so it is never taken."""
    if not sid or not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z0-9_-]+", sid):
        return ""
    best = ("", -1, "")
    for path in glob.glob(os.path.join(glob.escape(config_dir()), "projects", "*", sid + ".jsonl")):
        try:
            with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
                lines = f.readlines()
        except OSError:
            continue
        for n, line in enumerate(lines):
            try:
                e = json.loads(line)
            except ValueError:
                continue
            if not isinstance(e, dict) or e.get("type") != "assistant":
                continue
            ts = str(e.get("timestamp") or "")
            if since and ts and ts < since:
                continue
            text = message_text(e.get("message"))
            if RESULT_RX.search(text) and SHAPE_RX.search(text) and (ts, n) >= best[:2]:
                best = (ts, n, text)
    return best[2]


def harvest(log_path, since=None, rc=0, known=tuple(RELEASE_ON + STOP_ON), stops=tuple(STOP_ON)):
    """(result text, session id, RESULT word) from a `claude -p --output-format json` log.

    An end is clean only when the session exited 0 and its JSON flags no
    error. A nonzero exit or an error flag reads as ERROR (TIMEOUT for a
    session killed at its time limit), whatever its text says, and never takes
    the transcript fallback: a cut-off pass is never read as DONE. The one
    exception is a word in `stops` (such as BUDGET EXHAUSTED, printed by the
    session itself), which is kept, since stopping is the safe direction.
    `known` lists the multi-word verdicts read whole.

    `claude -p` prints only the last turn. A session whose last turn came after
    a background notification (a subagent finishing) can print a line with no
    RESULT in it while its real report sits earlier; then, on a clean end
    only, the transcript is read for the newest report-shaped text."""
    try:
        with open(log_path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
            s = f.read()
    except OSError:
        s = ""
    j = None
    try:
        j = json.loads(s[s.index("{"):])
        if not isinstance(j, dict):
            raise ValueError
        text, sid = str(j.get("result", "") or ""), str(j.get("session_id", "") or "")
    except (ValueError, AttributeError):
        j, text, sid = None, s[-4000:], ""
    flagged = bool(j and (j.get("is_error") or j.get("subtype") not in (None, "success")))
    word = result_word(text, known)
    if rc != 0 or flagged:
        why = f"rc={rc}" + (f", subtype={j.get('subtype')}" if j and j.get("subtype") else "") + \
              (", is_error" if j and j.get("is_error") else "")
        verdict = "TIMEOUT" if rc == TIMEOUT_RC else "ERROR"
        if word and word in stops:
            verdict = word
        return f"(an error end, {why}: never read as clean)\n{text}", sid, verdict
    if not word and sid:
        t = transcript_result(sid, since)
        if t:
            text = "(read from the session transcript: the last turn printed no RESULT line)\n" + t
            word = result_word(t, known)
    return text, sid, (word or "UNKNOWN")


def stamp_dir(root, label):
    stamp = now_utc().strftime("%H%M")
    parent = os.path.join(root, f"{label}-{stamp}")
    if os.path.exists(parent):
        parent += now_utc().strftime("%S")
    return parent


def session_argv(cfg, model, prompt, resume=None, session_id=None, files=(None, None)):
    """The launch line. A new session is given its id up front (--session-id),
    so a session killed before it prints anything still has a known id: the
    START line, the claim and the result file carry it, and `resume` can use it.
    `files` are the session's settings and user-instructions files
    (session_files): no user-level settings load, so the config is the grant."""
    argv = []
    if cfg["nice"]:
        argv += ["nice", "-n", str(cfg["nice"])]
    argv += [cfg["claude_bin"], "-p"]
    if resume:
        argv += ["--resume", resume]
    elif session_id:
        argv += ["--session-id", session_id]
    settings, instructions = files
    argv += ["--setting-sources", SETTING_SOURCES]
    if settings:
        argv += ["--settings", settings]
    if instructions:
        argv += ["--append-system-prompt-file", instructions]
    argv += ["--model", model, *cfg["claude_args"], "--output-format", "json", prompt]
    return argv


def session_env(cfg):
    """The launch environment: ours, the config's `env`, and the wait ceiling
    switched off, always (see CEILING_VAR)."""
    env = dict(os.environ)
    env.update({str(k): str(v) for k, v in cfg.get("env", {}).items()})
    env[CEILING_VAR] = "0"
    return env


def kill_group(p, grace=30):
    """End a session and everything it started: TERM to its process group,
    then KILL after `grace` seconds."""
    for sig, wait in ((signal.SIGTERM, grace), (signal.SIGKILL, 10)):
        try:
            os.killpg(p.pid, sig)
        except (ProcessLookupError, PermissionError):
            pass
        try:
            p.wait(timeout=wait)
            return
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            continue


def launch(cfg, argv, cwd, log):
    """Run one session to its end and return its exit code. A session still
    running at session_timeout_minutes (0: no limit) is ended, with its whole
    process group, and returns TIMEOUT_RC: a session that never exits would
    otherwise hold its slot, and the driver and its lock, past the reset."""
    limit = cfg.get("session_timeout_minutes") or 0
    # stdin from /dev/null, always: see the module docstring. Its own process
    # group, so a time limit ends everything it started.
    with open(log, "w") as out, open(log + ".err", "w") as err:
        p = subprocess.Popen(argv, cwd=cwd, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=out, stderr=err,
                             env=session_env(cfg), start_new_session=True)
        try:
            return p.wait(timeout=limit * 60 if limit else None)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            print(f"TIMEOUT {now_utc():%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ} the session ran past "
                  f"session_timeout_minutes={limit}; ending it", flush=True)
            kill_group(p)
            return TIMEOUT_RC
        except BaseException:
            kill_group(p, grace=5)  # a ^C to ccfleet ends its session too
            raise


def iso_now():
    return f"{now_utc():%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S}"


def read_at_file(v):
    """A variable written `@file:PATH` takes the file's text (a multi-line task)."""
    if isinstance(v, str) and v.startswith("@file:"):
        with open(expand(v[6:]), encoding="utf-8") as f:
            return f.read().rstrip("\n")
    return v


def safe(s):
    return re.sub(r"[^A-Za-z0-9._-]+", "-", s).strip("-")


def resolve_pin(cfg, facts):
    """(pin, source) for a repo: tools/pins.py's reading of its checkout, else
    the first atelier@<sha> in its CLAUDE.md on the mainline (or in the
    checkout), else ("UNRESOLVED", why). Atelier is vars.ATELIER, else the
    checkout this file lives in."""
    atelier = expand(cfg["vars"].get("ATELIER") or
                     os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))))
    pins = os.path.join(atelier, "tools", "pins.py")
    primary = facts["primary"]
    if os.path.isfile(pins):
        try:
            r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, pins, "--atelier", atelier, "--head", "--child", primary,
                                "--json"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120,
                               stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
            kids = json.loads(r.stdout).get("children") or []
            if kids and kids[0].get("pin"):
                return str(kids[0]["pin"]).lower(), "pins.py"
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError, ValueError, AttributeError):
            pass
    text = git("show", f"{facts['defref']}:CLAUDE.md", cwd=primary, check=False)
    if not text:
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(primary, "CLAUDE.md"), encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
                text = f.read()
        except OSError:
            text = ""
    m = PIN_RX.search(text)
    if m:
        return m.group(1).lower(), "CLAUDE.md"
    why = "pins.py found none" if os.path.isfile(pins) else f"no {pins}"
    return "UNRESOLVED", f"{why}, and CLAUDE.md has no atelier@<sha>"


def base_values(cfg, repo, facts, wt, by, extra, template_text, branch_name=None):
    """Every template value a session gets. {PIN} is resolved only when the
    template uses it and nothing supplied it; an unresolved pin is filled as
    UNRESOLVED and said, never left silently empty."""
    scratch = os.path.join(cfg["kit_dir"], "scratch", safe(repo))
    values = {**{k: str(v) for k, v in cfg["vars"].items()}, **{k: str(v) for k, v in extra.items()},
              "REPO": repo, "GH": facts["gh"], "BR": facts["branch"], "VIS": facts["visibility"],
              "WT": wt, "PRIMARY": facts["primary"], "LANDING": landing_text(facts, wt, branch_name),
              "NOTES": cfg["repos"].get(repo, {}).get("notes", "none"), "SCRATCH": scratch + "/",
              "BY": by, "DATE": f"{now_utc():%Y-%m-%d}"}
    if "PIN" not in values and "{PIN}" in template_text:
        values["PIN"], source = resolve_pin(cfg, facts)
        if values["PIN"] == "UNRESOLVED":
            print(f"PIN UNRESOLVED for {repo}: {source}; the prompt says UNRESOLVED", flush=True)
        else:
            print(f"PIN {values['PIN']} for {repo} (from {source})", flush=True)
    values.setdefault("PIN", "")
    return values


def read_template(path):
    with open(expand(path), encoding="utf-8") as f:
        return f.read()


def unlanded_work(wt, facts, branch_name=None):
    """Why removing `wt` would lose work, or "" when it holds none: uncommitted
    or untracked files, or commits on no remote branch (with no remote: on no
    branch but the worktree's own). A worktree that cannot be read holds work,
    as far as this check can tell."""
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "status", "--porcelain"], cwd=wt, capture_output=True, text=True)
    if r.returncode:
        return f"git status failed: {r.stderr.strip()[:200]}"
    if r.stdout.strip():
        n = len(r.stdout.strip().splitlines())
        return f"{n} uncommitted or untracked path(s)"
    if facts.get("remote"):
        not_on = ["--not", "--remotes"]
    else:
        not_on = ["--not", facts["branch"]]
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "rev-list", "--count", "HEAD", *not_on], cwd=wt, capture_output=True, text=True)
    if r.returncode:
        return f"git rev-list failed: {r.stderr.strip()[:200]}"
    if int(r.stdout.strip() or 0):
        where = "no remote branch" if facts.get("remote") else f"no branch but {branch_name or 'its own'}"
        return f"{r.stdout.strip()} commit(s) on {where}"
    return ""


def unlanded_verdict(wt, facts, branch_name, result, text):
    """(text, RESULT) for a session that ended with a clean RESULT: UNLANDED,
    with the reason put first in the text, when its worktree still holds work
    that is on no remote branch; else both unchanged. An UNLANDED end is not
    clean: no release, the worktree is kept, `resume` can finish it."""
    why = unlanded_work(wt, facts, branch_name)
    if not why:
        return text, result
    print(f"UNLANDED {wt}: the session said RESULT {result} but left {why}; read as not clean, so any "
          f"claim stays and the worktree is kept (land it, or resume the session)", flush=True)
    return (f"(RESULT {result} read as {UNLANDED}: the worktree {wt} holds {why}; never read as clean)\n"
            + text), UNLANDED


def remove_worktree(facts, wt, branch_name=None):
    """Remove a worktree only when it holds no unlanded work, never with
    --force; say why when it is kept. Returns True when it was removed."""
    why = unlanded_work(wt, facts, branch_name)
    if not why:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "worktree", "remove", wt], cwd=facts["primary"], capture_output=True, text=True)
        why = "" if r.returncode == 0 else f"git worktree remove refused: {r.stderr.strip()[:200]}"
    if why:
        print(f"KEPT worktree {wt}: {why}", flush=True)
        return False
    try:
        os.rmdir(os.path.dirname(wt))
    except OSError:
        pass
    if branch_name:
        git("branch", "-d", branch_name, cwd=facts["primary"], check=False)
    return True


def results_day():
    """The UTC day whose results folder a run writes: $CCFLEET_RESULTS_DAY
    (set by `drive` for its children, so one drive keeps one folder), else today."""
    d = os.environ.get("CCFLEET_RESULTS_DAY", "")
    if re.fullmatch(r"\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}", d):
        return d
    return now_utc().strftime("%Y-%m-%d")


RESULT_HEAD_RX = re.compile(r"^<!-- session (\S*) rc=(\S+) result=(.*?) -->")


def run_one(cfg, repo, template, label=None, model=None, extra=None, by="unknown",
            dry=False, keep=False, pre=None, post=None, check_file=None, existing=None,
            remove_adopted=False):
    """Make the worktree, render, launch, harvest, clean up. Returns (exit, RESULT word).

    The worktree is removed only when the RESULT is clean (queue.release_on),
    a `post` hook did not report a refused release, and the tree holds no
    unlanded work; otherwise it is kept and said. An adopted worktree (--in)
    is the caller's and is kept, unless `remove_adopted` (run --in --item: a
    fold, whose launcher puts the worktree away). A refused release exits 2."""
    if reset_reached(cfg):
        print(f"RESET reached; not starting {label or repo}")
        return EXIT_RESET, "RESET"
    check_repo_name(repo)
    extra = {k: read_at_file(v) for k, v in (extra or {}).items()}
    model = model or extra.pop("MODEL", None) or cfg["repos"].get(repo, {}).get("model") or cfg["default_model"]
    label = safe(label or repo)
    facts = repo_facts(cfg, repo, fetch=not dry)
    day = results_day()
    wt = expand(existing) if existing else os.path.join(stamp_dir(cfg["worktrees_root"], label), repo)
    if existing and (os.path.basename(wt) != repo or not os.path.isdir(wt)):
        # The basename is the billing key: a session in a differently named
        # directory would bill some other label.
        raise Refusal(EXIT_SETUP, f"--in must be an existing worktree whose basename is {repo}: {wt}")
    keep = keep or (bool(existing) and not remove_adopted)  # an adopted worktree is the caller's
    kit = cfg["kit_dir"]
    logs, results, scratch = (os.path.join(kit, "logs"), os.path.join(kit, "results", day),
                              os.path.join(kit, "scratch", safe(repo)))
    log = os.path.join(logs, os.path.basename(os.path.dirname(wt))
                       + (f"-in-{now_utc():%H%M%S}" if existing else "") + ".json")
    branch_name = None if (facts["remote"] or existing) else f"fleet/{label}-{now_utc().strftime('%H%M%S')}"
    sid0 = str(uuid.uuid4())
    template_text = read_template(template)
    values = base_values(cfg, repo, facts, wt, by, {**extra, "SESSION": sid0}, template_text, branch_name)
    prompt = build_prompt(cfg, template, values, template_text)
    argv = session_argv(cfg, model, prompt, session_id=sid0, files=session_files(cfg, sid0, write=False))
    clean = release_on(cfg)
    if dry:
        print(f"DRY {repo} model={model} vis={facts['visibility']} wt={wt}")
        print(f"DRY worktree add from {facts['defref']}; log={log}")
        print("DRY argv: " + " ".join(argv[:-1]) + f" <prompt {len(prompt)} chars> < /dev/null")
        # The same END line a real run prints, so a dry drain reads its passes
        # as ended rather than "ended early".
        print(f"END {now_utc():%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ} rc=0 result=DRY session=- out={log}", flush=True)
        return 0, "DRY"
    # Written before the worktree or the claim: a user settings file that
    # cannot be read refuses the launch with nothing to undo.
    argv = session_argv(cfg, model, prompt, session_id=sid0, files=session_files(cfg, sid0))
    for d in (logs, results, scratch, os.path.dirname(wt)):
        os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
    if existing:
        pass
    elif facts["remote"]:
        git("worktree", "add", "-q", "--detach", wt, facts["defref"], cwd=facts["primary"])
    else:
        git("worktree", "add", "-q", "-b", branch_name, wt, facts["branch"], cwd=facts["primary"])

    def cleanup():
        if not existing:
            remove_worktree(facts, wt, branch_name)

    if check_file and not os.path.isfile(os.path.join(wt, check_file)):
        print(f"not on {facts['defref']}: {check_file}")
        cleanup()
        return EXIT_SETUP, "UNKNOWN"
    if pre:
        code = pre(values, log)
        if code:
            cleanup()
            return code, "UNKNOWN"
    for line in repo_grants(wt):
        print(f"WIDENED by the repo's own {line} (it loads with the project; man ccfleet, THREATS)", flush=True)
    print(f"START {now_utc():%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ} {label} model={model} session={sid0} wt={wt} log={log}",
          flush=True)
    since = iso_now()
    rc = launch(cfg, argv, wt, log)
    text, sid, result = harvest(log, since, rc, known=clean + stop_on(cfg), stops=stop_on(cfg))
    sid = sid or sid0
    if result in clean and (remove_adopted or not existing):
        # The principal, 2026-11-23: a clean RESULT that leaves unlanded work
        # is not clean. The claim stays and the pass can be resumed; only an
        # adopted worktree with no item (the caller's own) is not judged.
        text, result = unlanded_verdict(wt, facts, branch_name, result, text)
    out = os.path.join(results, f"{label}.md")
    with open(out, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        f.write(f"<!-- session {sid} rc={rc} result={result} -->\n{text}\n")
    print(f"END {now_utc():%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ} rc={rc} result={result} session={sid} out={out}", flush=True)
    released = True
    if post:
        released = post(values, result, sid, log) is not False
    if keep:
        pass
    elif result in clean and released:
        remove_worktree(facts, wt, branch_name)
    else:
        why = "the release was refused" if not released else f"RESULT {result} is not clean"
        print(f"KEPT worktree {wt}: {why}", flush=True)
    return (0 if released else EXIT_CLAIM), result


